  ],
  "argument": "The evaluation framework exhibits severe physical and domain-specific implausibilities that undermine its validity. First, a storm catalog radius of 0.00 km is physically impossible; tropical cyclones and associated convective features have minimum physical dimensions of tens to hundreds of kilometers. Setting a zero-radius target guarantees physical and mathematical failure regardless of algorithm quality. Second, the spatial scales involved are physically incompatible. A median extraction radius of 22.1 km is characteristic of individual convective cells, whereas the actual feature locations for storms like SETH (315.1 km away) and HOLA (247.7 km away) are on the synoptic scale. At distances exceeding 200 km, any extracted features cannot physically represent the core of the cataloged storm center and are likely unrelated ambient convective systems. Applying a tight median radius to features that naturally span hundreds of kilometers represents a failure to align the extraction mechanics with the physical dimensions of meteorological phenomena.",
  "dissent": false,
